{"name":"Bite My Tongue","hex":"#d47d72","fetched":"2026-10-11","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"bite","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To cut into something by clamping the teeth.","To hold something by clamping one's teeth."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The act of biting.","The wound left behind after having been bitten."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/bite"},{"term":"tongue","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The flexible muscular organ in the mouth that is used to move food around, for tasting and that is moved into various positions to modify the flow of air from the lungs in order to produce different sounds in speech.","Such an organ, as taken from animals and used for food (especially from cows)."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["On a wind instrument, to articulate a note by starting the air with a tap of the tongue, as though by speaking a 'd' or 't' sound (alveolar plosive).","To manipulate with the tongue."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/tongue"}],"entries":[{"title":"Bite My Tongue","blocks":[{"text":"\"Bite My Tongue\" is the second single from You Me at Six's third studio album Sinners Never Sleep, released on 4 December 2011 as a digital download in the United Kingdom. The single reached number 124 on the UK Singles Chart and number 10 on the UK Rock & Metal Singles Chart. The song features guest vocals from Oliver Sykes of Bring Me the Horizon."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/5/50/BiteMyTongue.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bite_My_Tongue"},{"title":"Biting","blocks":[{"text":"Biting is an action involving a set of teeth closing down on an object. It is a common zoological behavior, being found in toothed animals such as mammals, reptiles, amphibians, fishes, and arthropods. Biting is also an action humans participate in, most commonly when chewing food. Myocytic contraction of the muscles of mastication is responsible for generating the force that initiates the preparatory jaw abduction (opening), then rapidly adducts (closes) the jaw and moves the top and bottom teeth towards each other, resulting in the forceful action of a bite. Biting is one of the main functions in the lives of larger organisms, providing them the ability to forage, hunt, eat, build, play, fight, protect, and much more. Biting may be a form of physical aggression due to predatory or territorial intentions. In animals, biting can also be a normal activity, being used for eating, scratching, carrying objects, preparing food for young, removing ectoparasites or irritating foreign objects, and social grooming. Humans can have the tendency to bite each other whether they are children or adults."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ec/Lion_Biting_Brother%27s_Tail_%2821277465378%29.jpg/330px-Lion_Biting_Brother%27s_Tail_%2821277465378%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Biting"},{"title":"Tongue","blocks":[{"text":"The tongue is a muscular organ in the mouth of a typical tetrapod. It manipulates food for chewing and swallowing as part of the digestive process and is the primary organ of taste. The tongue's upper surface (dorsum) is covered by taste buds housed in numerous lingual papillae. It is sensitive and kept moist by saliva and is richly supplied with nerves and blood vessels. The tongue also serves as a natural means of cleaning the teeth. A major function of the tongue is to enable speech in humans and vocalization in other animals."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b8/%D8%B2%D8%A8%D8%A7%D9%86_tongue.jpg/330px-%D8%B2%D8%A8%D8%A7%D9%86_tongue.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tongue"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/56/NIE_1905_Lion_-_teeth.jpg/330px-NIE_1905_Lion_-_teeth.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/56/NIE_1905_Lion_-_teeth.jpg/330px-NIE_1905_Lion_-_teeth.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Teeth of a lion, showcasing the front canines, middle carnassials, and back molars","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ANIE_1905_Lion_-_teeth.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0f/Worker_ant_carrying_leaf.jpg/500px-Worker_ant_carrying_leaf.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0f/Worker_ant_carrying_leaf.jpg/1280px-Worker_ant_carrying_leaf.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0f/Worker_ant_carrying_leaf.jpg/500px-Worker_ant_carrying_leaf.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"An ant carrying a leaf by biting it.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AWorker_ant_carrying_leaf.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/36/Human_bite_example_5.jpg/120px-Human_bite_example_5.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/36/Human_bite_example_5.jpg/250px-Human_bite_example_5.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/36/Human_bite_example_5.jpg/120px-Human_bite_example_5.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AHuman_bite_example_5.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9e/Human_bite_example_4.jpg/120px-Human_bite_example_4.jpg?